      "title": "Nevada asks the court to hold Kalshi in contempt and impose a $120,000-a-day geofencing fine",
      "text": "The Nevada Gaming Control Board told the First Judicial District Court in a Friday filing that Kalshi has failed to comply with the preliminary injunction barring it from offering sports, election and entertainment event contracts to people located in Nevada, and asked the court to hold the exchange in contempt with a $120,000 daily fine. Under a 24 July joint stipulation Kalshi agreed to implement geofencing by 12 August; missing that deadline triggers either the daily payment or a sworn explanation. NGCB says its investigators repeatedly accessed Kalshi from inside Nevada and bought prohibited contracts, including markets tied to NBA playoff games, MLB games, boxing, tennis and a celebrity wedding. Regulators argue Kalshi first relied on an address block and then \"a homegrown system based largely on IP addresses,\" which cannot reliably establish physical location and is trivially bypassed, and that the company must deploy commercial-grade geolocation of the kind every licensed US sportsbook already runs. Nevada is also seeking sanctions for every day of non-compliance plus reimbursement of NGCB costs and attorney fees. Judge Jason Woodbury has already found Kalshi's products fall within Nevada's definitions of a sports pool and a percentage game, rejecting at the injunction stage the argument that CFTC oversight categorically preempts state gaming enforcement. Kalshi disputes that conclusion and maintains it has taken adequate steps to comply.",
