      "title": "Albania prepares up to 10 online sports betting licences after seven years of prohibition",
      "text": "Albania is partially reopening its sports betting market after a blanket ban in force since 2019, with up to 10 online licences available; in July the Gambling Supervision Authority confirmed all eight implementing regulations had been finalised, more than two years after the enabling law passed. Gaming Compliance International estimates Albania's unregulated online sports betting GGR at $117m in 2024 rising to $126m in 2025, with total unregulated online GGR including casino and poker reaching an estimated $229m last year. Before the 2018 parliamentary vote to ban betting, the market generated annual turnover of EUR700m with 4,000 betting shops serving a population of just over 2.8 million; Prime Minister Edi Rama told parliament in October 2018: \"We are waging a frontal war with the evil entrenched deeply in our society over the years.\" Only online betting will be permitted - retail shops and slot halls remain barred, and casinos are restricted to five-star hotels. The government forecasts around ALL2bn (EUR20m) in annual public revenues, with 15% of GGR going to a special fund for culture, sport, technology and innovation alongside a 15% corporation tax. Licence fees are bid-determined with an ALL400m (EUR4m) minimum, paid in instalments over a 10-year term. Applicants must be Albanian joint-stock companies with gambling experience in at least three EU or OECD markets, turnover of at least EUR20m and EUR400,000 share capital, scored on a points system weighted 30 for fee bid, 30 for experience, 20 for governance, 15 for technology and five for business plan. Hendri Hanaj of Hashtag Lawyers said: \"There are a lot of conditions to get the licence and no Albanian company, at least, would be able to meet them.\"",
