      "title": "The UK regulator says operator identity data, not the CRA match, broke the affordability pilot",
      "text": "In a blog post published on Wednesday, the Gambling Commission gave further detail on its 2024-25 financial risk assessments pilot, with senior executives Helen Rhodes and Sarah Webster identifying deficiencies in operator identity verification as a key reason the pilot underdelivered. Incomplete or inaccurate personal details captured at onboarding meant some customers could not be matched to credit reference agency records - the match the Commission considers essential to a frictionless assessment. Recurring problems included initials used instead of full names, nicknames replacing legal forenames, and commercial rather than residential addresses; the same defects impair GAMSTOP protection and raise fraud and money laundering risk. The Commission reminded operators that Licence Condition 17 requires name, address and date of birth all to be verified as belonging to the same individual before gambling is permitted, and said more than 25% of complaints to its Contact Centre relate to identity verification - a leading cause of disputes escalated to ADR. It also criticised operators for deferring verification until withdrawal. Outgoing executive director Tim Miller confirmed at iGB L!VE that further FRA data will not be published until September, and BGC CEO Grainne Hurst said the checks \"cannot be described as genuinely frictionless if they produce unreliable outcomes.\"",
