    "title": "Finland's Supreme Administrative Court overturns spin-by-spin taxation of non-EEA online slots",
    "meta": "SBC News | 18 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "Finland's Supreme Administrative Court has struck down the Tax Administration's practice of treating every individual spin on a non-EEA online slot as a separate taxable event, voting 3-2 to require the tax authority to change its approach; taxable income must now be determined per session of play.",
    "why": "The taxable unit for player winnings is a platform data question before it is a tax question - session boundaries have to be defined, logged and reportable, and no bonus or gameplay engine built around spin-level events produces that shape of record natively.",
    "expanded": "The ruling, reported by Finnish outlet Taloustaito, applies to slot games operated outside the European Economic Area such as those based in Curacao. Miika Harkonen, tax lawyer at the Finnish Taxpayers' Association, said the crux was that \"the Supreme Administrative Court did not accept the Tax Administration's view that each game should be examined as a completely separate event.\" Antti Koivula, Chief Compliance Officer at Hippos ATG, called the previous approach \"absurd\" but flagged that the win is partial: taxable income is now set per session, yet \"losses from one session cannot be offset against profits from another, meaning that a slot player may still lose money overall while owing considerable tax.\" The ruling was unpublished. Koivula added that a separate but related ruling on professional sports betting is expected imminently. The Tax Administration must now update its policies. The timing matters because Finland's licensed market opens from 1 July 2027, with affiliates barred from operating in Finland from that date.",
