    "expanded": "Of the 3.97 million participants, 249,000 people - 6.3% of participants - met clinical criteria consistent with problem gambling. Among adolescents who gambled, 7% met problem-gambling criteria, split 9.7% of males against 1.5% of females, compared with 6.1% of adult gamblers. The survey distinguished carefully between meeting problem-gambling criteria and a clinical gambling disorder diagnosis, which the World Health Organization recognises as a mental disorder in ICD-11. Guillermo Penaloza Solano, psychiatrist at Mexico's Comision Nacional de Salud Mental y Adicciones, drew the behavioural line: \"La diferencia entre una apuesta ocasional y un trastorno por juego de apuestas es que la persona sigue apostando aun cuando ya enfrenta perdidas.\" He noted that digital platforms can reinforce this pattern by enabling continuous betting from a mobile phone with immediate rewards - a design critique that maps directly onto session-length and reward-cadence decisions in product. The survey is the first of its kind in Mexico and gives regulators a baseline they previously lacked.",
